{"description":"We're on the path to a new kind of web, with an uncertain future: AI-based user agents are fast-becoming a core interface to the internet. Platforms have increasingly seized control of the publisher-reader relationship: search engines are squeezing out web publishers to juice ad revenue, while social platforms have captured audiences with algorithmic reach and paid promotion.\nHow can publishers engage in this platform shift beyond being a dataset to be scraped? How can we avoid being trapped by a new wave of mega-platforms? And how can publishers on the web reach real users, build lasting relationships, and continue to have a equitable business model?\nJoin us for an evening of presentations and open discussion, where protocol builders and publishers share their thinking about the future of the web. You will hear short talks about opportunities in protocol-based publishing that give you control over your content including leveraging AI ethically, new monetization methods, and media provenance across social media. Each talk will include a short Q&A. Following will be a relaxed mixer over drinks and light bites with the speakers and peers.\nLearn more about PfP✨ and sign up to the mailing list: https://protocolsforpublishers.com\n\nSpeaker lineup\nOur evening will be MC'd by Jake Hirsch-Allen from The Dais, featuring the following speakers:\nThe Green Line publisher Anita Li and head of product and growth Temitayo Akinyemi on how can independent newsrooms build modern, audience-centred products and sustainable business models without relying on Silicon Valley’s platform dominance.\nViolet Cortes and Cole Capilongo of Hypha survey the state of the art in digital provenance—techniques for tracing a digital asset's lifecycle—and demo a tool that traces image histories on Bluesky, adding context to the scroller's experience.\nSeher Shafiq, global community programming lead at Mozilla Foundation will be discussing the use of open weight AI models and the alternatives to large centralized models.\nNikita Roy, founder of Newsroom Robots Lab and co-author of The Science of First: Catching Early Signals Before They Become News, will explore emerging approaches to value exchange, access and monetization in an agentic ecosystem, and consider the strategic questions news organizations should be thinking about as the landscape evolves.\nLearn more about each speaker at the PfP✨Toronto event page →\nAnd don't forget to apply for the Stakeholder Forum on Tuesday, October 6th\nAgenda\n6:30 Doors open and networking\n7:00 Opening remarks\n7:10 Talks\n8:20 Networking\n9:00 Last Call\n9:30 fin\n🍹🥂🍷 There will be a cashless bar so be sure to have a form of credit on hand.\nSponsors\nPfP✨Toronto is convened with the support of:","organizer":"Protocols for Publishers"}